WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
How can a cell halve its chromosome sets without losing any chromosome type?

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.640
Watch this cell carry a large pair and a small pair.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.000
Green chromosomes came from one parent.

4
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.120
Orange chromosomes came from the other.

5
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.560
Every pair carries the same kinds of genes.

6
00:00:19.683 --> 00:00:22.723
The partners in each pair are homologous chromosomes.

7
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:28.063
They carry genes in matching positions, but their alleles may differ.

8
00:00:29.363 --> 00:00:31.183
An allele is a version of a gene.

9
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:37.383
Matching does not mean identical; homologs can carry different instructions for a

10
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:42.963
trait. The starting cell is diploid because it has both members of every pair.

11
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.293
Before meiosis, each chromosome copies its DNA.

12
00:00:48.793 --> 00:00:51.273
Each rod becomes joined sister chromatids.

13
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:55.973
The DNA amount doubled, but the chromosome count did not.

14
00:00:57.253 --> 00:01:00.653
An X shape shows a copied chromosome, not a homologous pair.

15
00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:06.673
Count joined sister chromatids as one chromosome until their connection separates.

16
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:10.517
Now the homologous chromosomes meet as pairs.

17
00:01:11.877 --> 00:01:15.517
In the first division, each homolog moves away from its partner.

18
00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:20.537
The green long chromosome can travel with the orange short chromosome.

19
00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:24.557
The other homologs travel to the opposite side.

20
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:30.407
Each new cell now has a member of every homologous pair, still made of joined

21
00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:33.987
sisters. What must separate in the next division?

22
00:01:35.307 --> 00:01:37.627
Follow the yellow connection in each X shape.

23
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:45.823
The sister chromatids separate next.

24
00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:49.743
No DNA copying happens between the divisions.

25
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:54.323
Each cell divides again as the joined copies pull apart.

26
00:01:55.623 --> 00:01:59.543
The finished cells hold a single chromosome from every original pair.

27
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:04.623
These cells are haploid because each has a single chromosome set.

28
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:10.483
In the simple animal model, the completed divisions usually produce a group of

29
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:16.683
haploid cells. Human egg formation is uneven, and plants make spores instead of

30
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:21.103
gametes. Those refinements do not change the separation rule.

31
00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:26.577
Meiosis separates homologous pairs first, then sister chromatids.

32
00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:32.177
The first division separates homologous pairs and halves the chromosome sets.

33
00:02:33.537 --> 00:02:37.497
The second separates the sister chromatids of each copied chromosome.

34
00:02:38.817 --> 00:02:44.097
This is not mitosis repeated, because mitosis never separates homologous pairs.

35
00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:49.987
When egg and sperm later join, their haploid sets restore a diploid cell.

36
00:02:51.407 --> 00:02:54.347
Halving keeps chromosome number from doubling each generation.

37
00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:58.227
The products can carry different chromosome mixes.

38
00:02:59.647 --> 00:03:03.267
The next film asks why those products carry different allele mixes.
